The case \(n{<}0\) can be reduced to the case \(\sin^{2}a{<}N{<}1\) by writing

**17.7.15**: \[\begin{array}{l}N{=}(\sin^{2}\alpha{-}n)(1{-}n)^{-1}\\ p_{z}{=}[{-}n(1{-}n)^{-1}(\sin^{2}\alpha{-}n)]^{\sharp}\\ \end{array}\]
**17.7.16**: \[\begin{array}{l}[(1{-}n)(1{-}n^{-1}\sin^{2}\alpha)]^{\sharp}\Pi(n;\,\varphi \backslash\alpha)\\ =[(1{-}N)(1{-}N^{-1}\sin^{2}\alpha)]^{\sharp}\Pi(N;\,\varphi\backslash\alpha)\\ +\,p_{z}^{-1}\sin^{2}\alpha F(\varphi\backslash\alpha){+}\arctan\,\left[1{\frac {1}{2}}p_{z}\,\sin\,2\varphi/\Delta(\varphi)\right]\\ \end{array}\]
**17.7.17**: \[\begin{array}{l}\Pi(n\backslash\alpha){=}({-}n\cos^{2}\alpha)(1{-}n)^{-1}( \sin^{2}\alpha{-}n)^{-1}\Pi(N\backslash\alpha)\\ +\sin^{2}\alpha(\sin^{2}\alpha{-}n)^{-1}K(\alpha)\\ \end{array}\]
**Numerical Methods**

### 17.8 Use and Extension of the Tables

**Example 1.** Reduce to canonical form \(\int y^{-}dx\), where

\[y^{\sharp}{=}{-}3x^{\sharp}{+}34x^{\flat}{-}119x^{\flat}{+}172x{-}90\]

By inspection or by solving an equation of the fourth degree we find that

\[y^{\sharp}{=}Q_{\sharp}Q_{\sharp}\,\mbox{where}\,\,\,Q_{\sharp}{=}3x^{\flat}{ -}10x{+}9,\,Q_{\sharp}{=}{-}x^{\sharp}{+}8x{-}10\]

\[Q_{\sharp}{-}\lambda Q_{\sharp}{=}(3+\lambda)x^{\flat}{-}(10+8\lambda)x{+}9+10\lambda\]

is a perfect square if the discriminant

\[\begin{array}{l}\mbox{Special Cases}\\ \mbox{$17.7.18$}\hskip 14.226378ptn{=}0\\ \Pi(0;\,\varphi\backslash\alpha){=}F(\varphi\backslash\alpha)\\ 17.7.19\hskip 14.226378ptn{=}0,\,\alpha{=}0\\ \Pi(0;\,\varphi\backslash 0){=}\varphi\\ \end{array}\]

By inspection or by solving an equation of the fourth degree we find that

\[y^{\sharp}{=}Q_{\sharp}Q_{\sharp}\,\mbox{where}\,\,\,Q_{\sharp}{=}3x^{\flat}{ -}10x{+}9,\,Q_{\sharp}{=}{-}x^{\sharp}{+}8x{-}10\]

and then

\[Q_{\sharp}{+}\frac{2}{3}\,\,Q_{\sharp}{=}\frac{7}{3}\,\,(x{-}1)^{\sharp},\,Q_ {\sharp}{-}\frac{1}{2}\,\,Q_{\sharp}{=}\frac{7}{3}\,\,(x{-}2)^{\sharp}\]

Solving for \(Q_{\sharp}\) and \(Q_{\sharp}\) we get

\[Q_{\sharp}{=}(x{-}1)^{\sharp}{+}2(x{-}2)^{\sharp},\,Q_{\sharp}{=}2(x{-}1)^{ \sharp}{-}3(x{-}2)^{\sharp}\]

The substitution \(t{=}(x{-}1)/(x{-}2)\) then gives

\[\int y^{-}dx{=}\pm\int[(t^{\sharp}{+}2)(2t^{\sharp}{-}3)]^{\sharp}d\tau\]